  <article class="almanac-prose">
    <section id="handle" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>06.1 · one handle, no bytes</span>
        <h2>The guest gets a handle, never a path.</h2>
        <p>Edit an 8 MiB photo from Slack with gpt-image and the picture never enters the model context, a JSON frame, or the Wasm guest’s memory. The proposal names it as <code>chat-asset:3</code>; the host does the rest.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="one-tool-contract">
        <div><small>in the proposal</small><strong><code>chat-asset:3</code></strong></div>
        <div><small>in the guest</small><strong><code>handle { info, read, read-at }</code></strong></div>
        <div><small>on the wire</small><strong><code>SCM_RIGHTS</code> on the frame’s first byte</strong></div>
      </div>

      <p><code>open("chat-asset:3")</code> resolves only the descriptors this invocation delivered, in the order the proposal named them. The 32-row conversation table rides every request as metadata; at most 5 files cross, and they are the ones this proposal asked for.</p>

      <div class="article-callout callout-decision">
        <strong>The shell still holds no file descriptor.</strong>
        <p>The one-tool runtime’s “no host file descriptors” rule is unchanged. A handle is a WIT resource with three methods; the descriptor lives in the broker host and every read is a positional <code>pread</code> bounded to 64 KiB.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="hops" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>06.2 · seven hops, zero copies</span>
        <h2>The descriptor rides with the request.</h2>
      </div>

      <ol class="identity-chain" aria-label="One chat image crossing from the gateway to a provider and back">
        <li><span>01</span><div><small>gateway</small><strong>Spools the upload to its own private disk</strong><p>One row in the conversation table: number, content-type, byte count, SHA-256. 256 MiB LRU per gateway.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>02</span><div><small>model</small><strong>Proposes <code>image edit --image chat-asset:3</code></strong><p><code>run-command</code> is pure; the reference is a string leaf, and a <code>data:</code> URL anywhere in a proposal is refused before any fetch.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>03</span><div><small>gateway</small><strong>Opens the file fresh, read-only, close-on-exec</strong><p>Never the spool’s own read-write descriptor. Distinct references, first-occurrence order, one descriptor each; 40 MiB decoded is the ceiling.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>04</span><div><small>socket</small><strong><code>sendmsg</code> carries the fds with the frame’s first byte</strong><p>The 4-byte length prefix is read with <code>recvmsg</code>, so the descriptors and the frame arrive together. Linux sets <code>MSG_CMSG_CLOEXEC</code>; macOS gets <code>fcntl(FD_CLOEXEC)</code> before any other code sees the fd.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>05</span><div><small>broker</small><strong>Admits or refuses before a policy decision</strong><p>Four checks: regular file, <code>O_RDONLY</code>, <code>fstat</code> length equal to the row’s byte count, and count equal to the references. Anything else is <code>InvalidDescriptor</code>: a directory, a socket, a pipe, or a read-write fd. Fds on any frame but <code>Invoke</code> are refused and closed.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>06</span><div><small>host</small><strong>Streams the body and hashes what it sent</strong><p>No broker-side copy. The HTTP host composes an exact-length body from literal parts and asset parts, re-encoding on the fly, and records the SHA-256 of the bytes that went out.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>07</span><div><small>broker → gateway</small><strong><code>attach</code> reopens read-only, unlinks, and passes the fd back</strong><p>The output is a writer in the broker’s <code>0700</code> directory; after attach there is no path anywhere for anyone to reopen. The gateway admits the path-less blob with <code>fstat</code>, no copy, and reclaims it with <code>close</code>.</p></div></li>
      </ol>

      <p><code>attach</code> puts a file in the conversation’s temp folder. <code>asset send</code> marks it for this turn’s reply, needs its own <code>asset.send</code> grant, and is capped at four per turn. Nothing is delivered on a trap, a denial, or a timeout.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="invariants" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>06.3 · kernel, host, config</span>
        <h2>Each invariant names what enforces it.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table class="almanac-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Property</th><th scope="col">Enforced by</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Read-only by construction</th><td><strong>kernel</strong> — every descriptor that crosses is <code>O_RDONLY</code>; the peer cannot write through it.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">No reopen after attach</th><td><strong>kernel</strong> — the broker unlinks before the fd leaves; the gateway UID never had a path.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Close-on-exec before exposure</th><td><strong>kernel</strong> — <code>MSG_CMSG_CLOEXEC</code> on Linux, atomic; <code>fcntl</code> on macOS, immediate.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">No shared cursor</th><td><strong>host</strong> — every read is positional; nothing calls <code>read(2)</code> or <code>lseek</code> on an asset descriptor.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">No path or fd in the guest</th><td><strong>host</strong> — <code>open</code> resolves this invocation’s references only; anything else is <code>unknown-reference</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Digest of what was sent</th><td><strong>host</strong> — hashed while streaming, recorded on <code>provider.invoke</code> with id, content-type, and byte count. Never bytes.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>attach</code> ≠ <code>send</code></th><td><strong>grant</strong> — <code>asset: { attach, remove, send }</code> is the fourth optional grant beside http, storage, and secret_use. <code>send</code> is external-write only.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Broker root</th><td><strong>config</strong> — <code>assets.rootPath</code> must be <code>0700</code> and owned by the broker; it is emptied on every boot.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="ceilings" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>06.4 · ceilings</span>
        <h2>Every limit is a number.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table class="almanac-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Limit</th><th scope="col">Value</th><th scope="col">Refusal</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">decoded bytes per asset</th><td>8 MiB</td><td><code>too-large</code></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">files per invocation</th><td>5</td><td><code>too-many-assets</code></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">decoded bytes in per invocation</th><td>40 MiB</td><td><code>TooLarge</code>, before dispatch</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">decoded bytes out per invocation (writers + streamed parts)</th><td>40 MiB</td><td><code>too-large</code> / <code>request-too-large</code></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">conversation table rows</th><td>32</td><td><code>TooManyRows</code></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">new sends per turn</th><td>4</td><td><code>sends-exhausted</code></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">broker in-flight stored bytes</th><td><code>assets.maxInFlightBytes</code>, chart default 64 MiB</td><td><code>over-budget</code></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">broker asset volume</th><td><code>volumeSizes.brokerAssets</code>, 320Mi</td><td>kubelet eviction, not <code>ENOSPC</code></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>Content limits count decoded bytes; disk budgets count stored bytes. One 8 MiB image stored as base64 occupies 11,184,812 bytes on disk.</p>

      <div class="article-callout callout-decision">
        <strong>Repeats are charged, not deduplicated.</strong>
        <p>A streamed body may list the same borrowed handle in every part. Only literal parts used to count against the request ceiling, so one 8 MiB handle listed 4,096 times composed a single exact-length 45.8 GB POST carrying the operator’s credential. Now every asset occurrence, replayed response handles included, is charged to the invocation’s 40 MiB counter before dispatch. Failed attempts don’t refund.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="one-kernel" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>06.5 · one kernel, one pod, one replica</span>
        <h2>The broker can’t scale out; that’s the price.</h2>
        <p>The usual cloud-native answer is to put the broker behind a Service and add replicas. That answer is unavailable here on purpose. A file descriptor is an open file in one kernel, not a URL, and <code>SO_PEERCRED</code> is the whole identity story. There is no TCP transport in the protocol crate to fall back to.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table class="almanac-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Constraint</th><th scope="col">Consequence</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>SCM_RIGHTS</code> needs a shared kernel</th><td>Gateway and broker are one pod; the broker is a native sidecar. <a href="/deploy/kubernetes/#one-pod">See the topology table on the Kubernetes page</a>.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Isolation is two UIDs and a <code>0660</code> socket, not cryptography</th><td>The attestation is plain JSON. If something hostile already runs as the gateway’s user, you’ve lost.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Memory became disk</th><td>The broker’s <code>/tmp</code> is a 16 Mi tmpfs, so assets need a 320Mi disk-backed volume. On a Raspberry Pi that is SD-card writes.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>emptyDir.sizeLimit</code> is a kubelet eviction</th><td>Overrun restarts the pod; <code>maxInFlightBytes</code> is what keeps you under it.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Nothing survives a restart</th><td>Assets are the conversation’s temp files. The broker empties its root at boot and the gateway table is the only store.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Hashing is CPU</th><td>The first read of each handle hashes it end to end, up to 40 MiB of SHA-256 per invocation.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Linux first</th><td>macOS keeps a documented non-atomic close-on-exec window; neither daemon spawns children, so it holds.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Content-type is a label</th><td>A magic-byte mismatch logs one record and does not refuse. Formats are the agent’s job.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="article-callout callout-warning">
        <strong>Old attachment envelopes are refused.</strong>
        <p><code>providerAttachments</code> and <code>chatAssetInputs</code> no longer parse, even empty. A provider that still returns <code>attachments[].base64</code> or accepts a <code>data:</code> URL is refused after its call runs; every other provider keeps loading. Core, the asset-producing providers, and the chart land in one order.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="receipts" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>06.6 · receipts</span>
        <h2>Five 4 MiB edits, measured.</h2>
        <p>Lasting RSS rise 60 s after five consecutive 4 MiB edits, healthy OTLP, linux/arm64, median of three, 20 September 2026:</p>
      </div>

      <div class="one-tool-contract">
        <div><small>before, v0.17.0</small><strong>41.1 MB</strong></div>
        <div><small>after</small><strong>5.7 MB</strong></div>
        <div><small>frame encode, one 8 MiB asset</small><strong>22.4 MB → 478 B</strong></div>
      </div>

      <p>An 8 MiB edit trapped at the 64 MiB guest cap on v0.17.0. It now succeeds with a 12.4 MB guest peak and exactly one HTTP request whose <code>Content-Length</code> matches the body: 11,185,016 bytes.</p>

      <div class="article-callout callout-warning">
        <strong>The after-side guest is a measurement build.</strong>
        <p>The released gpt-image provider rejects references outright, so the after numbers come from a measurement-only component, one run per full-size case. The model leg is untouched: four fetched images still cost 134.5 MB in the gateway, and that is a separate change.</p>
      </div>

